<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=solar-cell">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Energy and power</p><h1 class="enc-title">Solar cell</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1839">1839</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Photovoltaic cell, PV cell</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A solar cell, or photovoltaic cell, is an electronic device that converts the energy of light directly into electricity through the photovoltaic effect; the French physicist Edmond Becquerel built the first in 1839. The first practical photovoltaic cell was publicly demonstrated at Bell Laboratories in 1954, and by the 1960s solar cells were the main power source of most Earth-orbiting satellites. Almost all commercial cells are made of crystalline silicon, and module prices, adjusted for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a>, fell from $96 a watt in the mid-1970s to 30 cents in 2018.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/solar-cell.jpg" alt="Solar cell" width="320" height="286" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solar_cell.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Edmond Becquerel demonstrated the photovoltaic effect experimentally and in 1839, at the age of 19, built the first photovoltaic cell in his father's laboratory. In a 20 February 1873 issue of Nature, Willoughby Smith described the effect of light on selenium during the passage of an electric current. In 1876 William Grylls Adams and Richard Evans Day constructed the first solid-state photovoltaic cell, and in 1883 Charles Fritts built another by coating selenium with a thin layer of gold to form the junctions; his device was only about 1 per cent efficient.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A solar cell is an electronic device whose electrical characteristics change when it is exposed to light. Its operation needs three things: the absorption of light, the separation of charge carriers of opposite types and their separate extraction to an external circuit. Most cells are made of semiconducting material, usually silicon, fabricated into a p-n junction, formed by doping one side p-type and the other n-type with small amounts of boron or phosphorus. A single silicon cell produces an open-circuit voltage of about 0.5 to 0.6 volts, so cells are joined into modules, and strings of modules into arrays, which give direct current that an inverter often converts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alternating-current">alternating current</a>.</p><p>Almost all commercial cells are of crystalline silicon, cut into wafers 160 to 240 micrometres thick. Thin-film cells, of which cadmium telluride is the main kind, use less material, while multi-junction cells stack layers that absorb different parts of the spectrum and were first developed for satellites.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The first prominent application was in space. Solar cells were proposed and flown on the Vanguard satellite in 1958 as an alternative to its battery, which allowed the mission to last longer without changes to the spacecraft. In 1959 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> launched Explorer 6, with large wing-shaped arrays of 9600 Hoffman solar cells, and such arrays became a common feature of satellites.</p><p>Costs stayed high through the 1960s because space users would pay for the best cells, and in 1971 a cell cost some $100,000 per watt. Terrestrial research grew with a programme of the US National Science Foundation that ran from 1969 to 1977 and with the Cherry Hill Conference of 1973. Adjusted for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a>, a solar module cost $96 per watt in the mid-1970s and 30 cents per watt in 2018. Solar cells are also used to power calculators and watches.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Solar cell” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1839</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Edmond Becquerel</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The first practical solar cell was demonstrated at Bell Laboratories in 1954.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/voltaic-pile">Electric battery<span>1800</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=solar-cell">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
